WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.358
Ternary search optimizes functions that strictly decrease to a single minimum and then strictly increase.

00:00:07.358 --> 00:00:13.175
Convex cost functions and Euclidean distance sums often follow this unimodal shape.

00:00:13.175 --> 00:00:17.800
Confirm there are no flat plateaus before applying ternary search.

00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:23.222
Place two interior probes at one-third and two-thirds of the current interval.

00:00:23.222 --> 00:00:29.094
Evaluating the objective at both points reveals which outer third cannot hold the optimum.

00:00:29.094 --> 00:00:32.813
Discard the worse side and keep the remaining two-thirds.

00:00:33.133 --> 00:00:38.027
Each comparison shrinks the interval length to two-thirds of its previous size.

00:00:38.027 --> 00:00:42.859
Eighty iterations reduce a massive coordinate range down to machine precision.

00:00:42.859 --> 00:00:47.133
The check function only needs to evaluate the cost at a single point.

00:00:47.467 --> 00:00:53.832
On discrete integer coordinates, integer division can cause the probes to collide when the range is small.

00:00:53.832 --> 00:00:57.975
Stop the ternary loop once the interval holds three or four integers.

00:00:57.975 --> 00:01:01.638
Finish by evaluating those few remaining candidates directly.
